    "narrative": "The daily efficiency model for Ghost Ride the WHIP highlights **Corbin Carroll** (87.14) as our premier asset for this slate. Facing Adams, **Corbin Carroll** benefits from a potent combination of a high-ceiling park environment and a favorable matchup against a pitcher with a 4.57 xERA. While **Mickey Moniak** (75.75) and **James Wood** (74.56) also provide significant value, **James Wood** is particularly intriguing; his 0.408 xwOBA and top-of-the-order positioning amplify his stolen base potential against the relatively slow delivery of Valdez. **Gabriel Moreno** (71.10) rounds out our top-tier tier, leveraging a strong platoon advantage to anchor the catcher spot.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision involved keeping **Griffin Conine** (48.73) on the bench. He was the final cut, narrowly missing out due to the stiff competition from **Jose Caballero** and **Cole Young**; while **Griffin Conine** possesses an attractive platoon edge, the efficiency drop-off against a high-caliber arm like Gausman left his projected output slightly below our starting threshold. We continue to monitor the status of **Tyler Soderstrom**, who remains sidelined on the IL, while **Vinnie Pasquantino** and **Travis Bazzana** remain relegated to the bench today due to unfavorable SP xERA metrics that suppress their daily outlooks. \n\nRisk management remains a priority given the volatility in late-September matchups. Managers should note that while **Trevor Story** and **Brice Turang** are starting, both are facing sub-4.00 xERA arms (Griffin and Nola), which caps their upside despite their utility roles. Furthermore, **Jose Caballero**'s score of 48.76 sits near the bottom of our active lineup, making him a primary candidate for replacement if a pinch-hit opportunity arises. We are operating with a clear focus on maximizing our stolen base floor\u2014highlighted by **James Wood** and **Austin Riley**\u2014to keep pace in this tight 5x5 race.",
